   "properties": {
    "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
   },
   "widgets_values": [
    "## 2-character dialogue · MiniMax H3 + HyperFlow 8-step\n\nTwo faces, two voices: each character's identity is bound to its own dialogue track.\n\n**What to swap in**\n- Asset Bin on Pulse Slate: point **Ada** and **Ben** at your two character images and describe each one. Ada must sit on the **left** and Ben on the **right**; every shot's visual keeps that layout.\n- The two LoadAudio nodes: one **full-length track per character**, both the length of the scene, on the same clock, with silence while the other character speaks. Set `duration_seconds` on Pulse Slate to that length (placeholder: 25.92 s = 622 frames = two 311-frame windows) and retime the shots so each one covers its lines.\n\n**How the timing is kept**\n- Each PulseVoice is `lip_sync` on `film_clock` and names its speaker (`@Ada` → S1, `@Ben` → S2), so every window receives exactly its own seconds of each track, and the prompt says whose voice is whose.\n- Pulse Render `audio_mode` is **lock_source**: each window's lip-sync mix is also written into H3's *target* audio latent and held there, so the real waveform drives the mouths at every step instead of only being described to the model. (`reference_only` is the old behaviour; `remix_source` lets the model re-voice it by `remix_strength`.)\n- Pulse Render `use_reference_audio` is **on**, so the finished film carries your recordings, not H3's decode of them. Wire a clean take into a PulseVoice's `final_audio` and that is what gets muxed, at exactly the same seconds, while `audio` keeps driving the mouth.\n\n**Model chain** (replaces TaoMate 3-step on the pruned checkpoint): non-pruned `ref2va` and `fl2va` int8_convrot → **HyperFlow LoRA Loader** → Shift 12/3. Pulse Slate samples with **8 steps · euler · scheduler `hyperflow` · cfg 1.0**; nothing else is valid with HyperFlow. Sparse attention is left out: HyperFlow is validated on dense attention.\n\n**First run:** Pulse Render `dry_run` is **on**. Read the report: two voices bound to @Ada / @Ben, ref2va windows, 8 steps. Then turn dry_run off.\n\n**Lip-sync correction (bypassed).** Only if lock_source still falls short: select the right-hand group and press Ctrl+B. **Pulse Lip-Sync Segment** cuts out one character at a time: only the frames where *their own* recording is audible (plus 0.25 s handles), only their half of the frame (`box`), and only their voice, relabelled 24→25 fps for LatentSync (which assumes 25 fps; without it the mouth drifts ~4 % per second). **Pulse Lip-Sync Paste** writes the corrected frames back at exactly those frames, feathered, and the Ben pass runs on Ada's result. Change the boxes if your characters are framed differently."
   ],
   "title": "Read me"
  },
